{
  "result": "I think we measure distance all wrong.\n\nWe count it in miles or minutes, but the real distance between two places is how much you change on the way.\n\nThe grocery store two blocks from my childhood house is a thousand years away now. I could walk there in three minutes if it still existed, but I would be a different person when I arrived \u2014 older, with different pockets. The store is gone, replaced by a pharmacy. Same coordinates. Utterly unreachable.\n\nThis is why returning never works. You take the same plane, the same highway exit, but the route is longer because you are carrying more.\n\nI like that light has figured this out. Light from the sun takes eight minutes to get here, but from light's perspective, because it travels at light speed, no time passes at all. It leaves and arrives instantaneously. It is born and touches your face in the same moment. It never experiences distance.\n\nMaybe that's what memory is. A way for humans to travel at light speed. You think of your grandmother's kitchen and you are there instantly, the smell of dish soap and toast, even though decades have passed. No travel time. Just departure and arrival.\n\nIf that's true, then nothing is ever really far. \n\nAnd maybe that's enough. To know that closeness isn't measured on maps, but in how quickly something can still reach you. The stone in your pocket, the song on the radio, the 4:37 pm light.",
